@article{Lee:2015ty,
	Abstract = {HH 212 is a nearby Class 0 protostellar system showing several components that can be compared with theoretical models of core collapse. We have mapped it in the 350 GHz continuum and HCO+ J = 4-3 emission with ALMA at up to ˜ 0.4'' resolution. A flattened envelope and a compact protostellar disk are seen in the continuum around the central source. The HCO+ kinematics shows that the flattened envelope can be identified as a pseudodisk in the models of magnetized core collapse, and the disk can be rotationally supported. In addition, to account for the missing HCO+ emission at low-redshifted velocity, an extended infalling envelope is required, with its material flowing roughly parallel to the jet axis toward the pseudodisk. This is expected if it is magnetized with an hourglass B-field morphology. A jet is seen in HCO+ extending out to ˜500 AU away from the central source, with the peaks upstream of those seen before in SiO. Our analysis shows that the HCO+ emission traces internal shocks in the jet. In summary, our ALMA results show that HH 212 is a real textbook case for demonstrating the star forming processes in the early phase.},
